  "caption": "Organic liquid drum and bass blended with chamber folk and slow-burn indie rock. Fingerpicked steel-string acoustic guitar and warm cello carry the song from the first bar, a steady piano ostinato and an unbroken drum groove hold it together, strings swell layer by layer. The verses stay sparse and intimate; fast rolling breakbeats with deep warm sub bass surge in underneath for every chorus; the bridge drops back to guitar, cello and voice; the final chorus is the fullest, and the outro returns to the fingerpicked guitar alone. Warm close-miked female lead vocal in slow half-time phrasing, long held notes, every word sung clearly, clear English diction. Wooden and earthy on the surface, a rush underneath, cinematic, hopeful, quietly strong",
  "seed": 11,
  "lyrics": "[Intro]\n\n[Verse 1]\nThey say you're wise beyond your years\nLike you were born already grown\nThey never saw how deep the roots went down\nTo find the water on your own\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nYou keep the shaking on the inside\nSo the leaves won't give you away\n\n[Chorus]\nSteady as a tree when the wind is high\nNobody sees how the summers ran dry\nSome years you had to grow up twice\nYou've got more rings than years\nMore rings than years\n\n[Verse 2]\nYou learned too soon the rain makes no promises\nSo you count the cost of every branch you grow\nYou never wait for kinder weather\nYou reach for light, however slow\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\nYou keep the shaking on the inside\nSo the leaves won't give you away\n\n[Chorus]\nSteady as a tree when the wind is high\nNobody sees how the summers ran dry\nSome years you had to grow up twice\nYou've got more rings than years\nMore rings than years\n\n[Bridge]\nEverybody calls you strong\nAnd then they let you carry on\nI'd rather count the rings with you\nAnd learn the cost of everything\n\n[Final Chorus]\nSteady as a tree when the wind is high\nNow I can see how the summers ran dry\nSome years you had to grow up twice\nYou've got more rings than years\nMore rings than years\n\n[Outro]\nMore rings than years\nAnd still you grow",
